  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 206 AM EST Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: The moist cyclonic flow will continue through tonight, \nbut the shortwave responsible for the widespread showers yesterday \nhas passed to the south, ending most of the synoptic forcing. A \nsubsidence inversion will form today and begin to lower, keeping the \nclouds trapped under it, but also decreasing the moisture depth. By \ntonight, the moisture depth should be too shallow to produce \nprecipitation. Overall, only a couple tenths of an inch of \nadditional snow accumulations are expected today. Tonight into \nMonday, the lowering inversion should drop below summit height and \ncause undercast to develop.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A potent ridge and associated area of surface high \npressure will dominate the weather this week, making it feel as if \nit were still mid-October. In a classic El Nino pattern, deep \ntroughing and strong surface lows over the Pacific have caused \ncontinuous southwest to westerly flow out west, flooding the Great \nPlains and western Canada with warm Pacific air. This has caused \ntemperature anomalies in the 20-30+ degree range. This airmass will \nbegin to arrive Monday, but clouds will likely remain stuck under \nthe inversion, so it will likely take until Tuesday to warm up. From \nthen through the end of the week, and now likely through the \nweekend, highs in the 50s will prevail, though 60 will be possible \nin some of the warmest areas. There will be a strong, persistent \nsubsidence inversion, and it will aided by the fact that \nsignificantly more surface heat will be lost at night than will be \nable to be replenished by the sun during the day. Radiational \ncooling will help lows drop into the 20s and 30s, around \nclimatological normals. Weak troughs will gradually erode the ridge \nfrom the north, but it looks like it will take through next weekend \nfor the ridge to get pushed to the south and east. Therefore, cooler \nconditions and chances for precipitation to return will likely not \noccur until the start of the following week.",
